Below is a short analysis of what’s going on, followed by a minimal two-hunk patch that stops the CanvasLayerRenderer from “re-using” another layer’s canvas when both layers happen to have the same CSS opacity and the same transform.  In OL ≥ 6.8.1, each canvas layer checks whether it can recycle the incoming `target` element; because by default all layers share the same class name and, in your case, the same opacity and transform, layer 2 ends up drawing into layer 1’s `<canvas>` (and skipping the clear), so the second layer completely “paints over” the first.  We prevent that by tagging each container with its owning layer and only ever re-using a container if it really belongs to *this* layer.

